Z||000000||David Gamache||01-17-2000||07:04 PM||||Brief recap. The car was reading high on the nox even after a tune up(plugs, wires, egr, filters, etc...) Turns out the parts stores had incorect information. All the stores computers said,"egr cannot be tested with vacuum". Well as it is it can be bench tested quite easily. Simply push up on the diaphram and plug the port with your finger and the diaphram should hold in a up position. If it holds it works. According to the parts places the egr can only be tested at 2500-3000 rpm on the vehicle with a continous vacuum. Not true. The obvious final verdect incorrect new egr. For anyone elese who may decide to replace an egr I recommend using a genuine AC/delco replacement. After the tune-up, delco egr and a new three stage low restriction cat(just for good measure)the car passed smog with flying colors and rated far below the State average requirements! Thanks for all the helpful feedback.||||reg||10
